### What is an API Gateway?

The API Gateway is the single public entry point for all client requests. It handles routing, authentication, rate limiting, and request aggregation so these cross-cutting concerns live in one place instead of being duplicated in every service.

```java
package com.bank.gateway;

import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.route.RouteLocator;
import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.route.builder.RouteLocatorBuilder;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

@Configuration
public class GatewayRoutesConfig {

    @Bean
    public RouteLocator bankRoutes(RouteLocatorBuilder builder) {
        return builder.routes()
            .route("payment-service", r -> r
                .path("/api/v1/payments/**")
                .filters(f -> f
                    .circuitBreaker(c -> c.setName("paymentCB"))
                    .requestRateLimiter(l -> l.setRateLimiter(redisRateLimiter())))
                .uri("lb://payment-service"))
            .route("account-service", r -> r
                .path("/api/v1/accounts/**")
                .uri("lb://account-service"))
            .build();
    }
}
```

```text
Expected behavior:
GET /api/v1/payments/PAY-001 -> routed to payment-service instance
GET /api/v1/accounts/SAV2001 -> routed to account-service instance
The lb:// prefix means load-balanced lookup through the registry.
```

### What is a Service Registry?

The Service Registry solves the problem that service instances come and go with scaling and deploys, so you cannot hardcode addresses. Each service registers itself on startup, and callers resolve the logical name.

```java
package com.bank.account;

import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;
import org.springframework.cloud.client.discovery.EnableDiscoveryClient;

@SpringBootApplication
@EnableDiscoveryClient
public class AccountServiceApplication {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(AccountServiceApplication.class, args);
    }
}
```

```text
Eureka dashboard:
account-service -> 192.168.1.10:8081, 192.168.1.11:8081 (2 instances)
payment-service -> 192.168.1.20:8082 (1 instance)
notification-service -> 192.168.1.30:8083 (1 instance)
```

### What is a Config Server?

The Config Server externalizes configuration so services read settings from one central, Git-backed server. You change a timeout once without rebuilding any service.

```yaml
# application-payment-service.yml (stored in Git, served by Config Server)
spring:
  config:
    import: configserver:http://config-server:8888

account-service:
  timeout: 3000
  retry:
    max-attempts: 3

fraud-check:
  enabled: true
  threshold: 100000.00
```

```text
Expected behavior:
Payment Service starts -> pulls config from config-server:8888
Changing account-service.timeout to 5000 in Git
-> All payment-service instances pick up new value on refresh.
```

### How does inter-service communication work?

Synchronous communication uses a declarative client like Feign where the caller needs an immediate answer. Asynchronous communication uses Kafka where the caller should not wait.

```java
package com.bank.payment;

import org.springframework.cloud.openfeign.FeignClient;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PathVariable;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PostMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestBody;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

// Synchronous: Feign client resolves account-service via registry
@FeignClient(name = "account-service")
public interface AccountClient {

    @PostMapping("/api/v1/accounts/{id}/debit")
    void debit(@PathVariable String id, @RequestBody DebitRequest request);

    @PostMapping("/api/v1/accounts/{id}/credit")
    void credit(@PathVariable String id, @RequestBody CreditRequest request);
}
```

```java
package com.bank.payment;

import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

// Asynchronous: publish event so Notification Service reacts later
@Service
public class PaymentEventPublisher {

    private final KafkaTemplate<String, PaymentEvent> kafkaTemplate;

    public PaymentEventPublisher(KafkaTemplate<String, PaymentEvent> kafkaTemplate) {
        this.kafkaTemplate = kafkaTemplate;
    }

    public void publishTransferCompleted(String paymentId, PaymentEvent event) {
        kafkaTemplate.send("payment-events", paymentId, event);
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Synchronous: POST account-service/api/v1/accounts/SAV2001/debit -> 200 OK
Asynchronous: payment-events topic receives PaymentEvent for PAY-001
```

### What is Database-per-Service?

Each service owns its tables and no other service connects to them. If the Payment Service needs the holder's name, it calls the Account Service or consumes events into a local read model.

```text
account-service database:
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ accounts                             │
  │ id | holder_name | balance | type    │
  │ SAV2001 | Rajesh | 500000.00 | SAV   │
  │ SAV2002 | Priya  | 250000.00 | SAV   │
  └─────────────────────────────────────┘

payment-service database:
  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ payments                                   │
  │ id | from_account | to_account | amount    │
  │ PAY-001 | SAV2001 | SAV2002 | 50000.00    │
  └───────────────────────────────────────────┘

No cross-service joins. No shared schema.
```

### What is a Circuit Breaker?

A circuit breaker wraps remote calls and tracks the failure rate over a sliding window. When failures cross the threshold, it flips from CLOSED to OPEN and every call fails instantly by invoking the fallback.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

import io.github.resilience4j.circuitbreaker.annotation.CircuitBreaker;
import io.github.resilience4j.bulkhead.annotation.Bulkhead;
import io.github.resilience4j.retry.annotation.Retry;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

@Service
public class ResilientAccountClient {

    private static final Logger LOG = Logger.getLogger(ResilientAccountClient.class.getName());

    @CircuitBreaker(name = "accountService", fallbackMethod = "debitFallback")
    @Retry(name = "accountService")
    @Bulkhead(name = "accountService")
    public void debit(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // restTemplate.postForEntity("http://account-service/...")
        LOG.info("Debit completed for account: " + accountId);
    }

    public void debitFallback(String accountId, BigDecimal amount, Throwable t) {
        LOG.warning("Circuit OPEN: queuing debit for " + accountId);
    }
}
```

```text
Circuit Breaker state transitions:
CLOSED (normal) -> failure rate > 50% -> OPEN (fail fast)
OPEN -> wait 60s -> HALF_OPEN (test a few calls)
HALF_OPEN -> calls succeed -> CLOSED
HALF_OPEN -> calls fail -> OPEN
```

### How does observability work?

Observability ties the system together with metrics, logs, and traces. Prometheus scrapes metrics, Grafana dashboards them, ELK aggregates structured logs, and Zipkin with a shared traceId shows the full request waterfall.

```java
package com.bank.observability;

import io.micrometer.core.instrument.Counter;
import io.micrometer.core.instrument.MeterRegistry;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class TransferMetrics {

    private final Counter transfersInitiated;
    private final Counter transfersCompleted;
    private final Counter transfersFailed;

    public TransferMetrics(MeterRegistry registry) {
        this.transfersInitiated = registry.counter("transfers.initiated");
        this.transfersCompleted = registry.counter("transfers.completed");
        this.transfersFailed = registry.counter("transfers.failed");
    }

    public void recordInitiated() { transfersInitiated.increment(); }
    public void recordCompleted() { transfersCompleted.increment(); }
    public void recordFailed()    { transfersFailed.increment(); }
}
```

```text
Expected Prometheus metrics:
transfers_initiated_total 1523
transfers_completed_total 1500
transfers_failed_total 23
```

### How do these components work together?

None of these components are optional extras. Without a registry you get brittle addresses, without a config server you get config drift, and without circuit breakers one slow dependency takes the whole system down.

```text
Request flow through all components:

Client -> API Gateway (auth, rate limit)
            |
            v
       Service Registry (resolve payment-service)
            |
            v
       Load Balancer (pick healthy instance)
            |
            v
       Payment Service
            |
            +--[Feign + CircuitBreaker]--> Account Service
            |                                    |
            +--[Kafka]--> Notification Service   |
            |                                    |
       Payment DB                          Account DB
            |
       Micrometer -> Prometheus -> Grafana (metrics)
       Logback -> ELK (logs)
       Micrometer Tracing -> Zipkin (traces)
```

### What are common mistakes with these components?

The most common mistake is treating these components as optional.

```java
package com.bank.antipattern;

// WRONG: Hardcoded service URL. Breaks when instances change.
public class WrongAccountClient {
    private static final String URL = "http://192.168.1.10:8081/api/v1/accounts";
    // If this instance goes down or IP changes, everything breaks.
}

// CORRECT: Use service discovery with logical name.
// @FeignClient(name = "account-service")
// The registry resolves the name to healthy instances.
```
